@node Why pop-test?
@chapter Why pop-test?

@itemize
@item
Catches mistakes early – Helps find problems before real users do.
@item
Test coverage with a real meaning - test your systems from head to toe
@item
Frees you and allows you to refactor and test your systems more easily
@item
Saves time – No need to test every little part manually, test on higher level.
@item
Easy to use – Just write simple rules, in a comfy DSL and @code{pop-test} does the rest!
@item
Fast and reliable – Built with Rust, a programming language known for speed and safety.
@end itemize

@node Motivation
@chapter Motivation

Imagine you're building a toy robot, but you don't need to understand all the small parts inside the robot to know if it works. You just need to press a button and see if it performs the task correctly.

A big advantage of this is that you test based on the output, not the code itself or implementation.

Of course this doesn't mean you shouldn't add some unit/property-based tests when useful. But these shouldn't be the bulk of your tests.

It's great when you change or improve your code (refactor), that you don’t need to worry about breaking anything as long as the system gives the same results as before.

It's kind of like saying: "As long as pressing the button still works the same, everything’s fine."

These tests are a safety net for changes to systems (specially if you aren't too familiar with the system yet). It gives you confidence when making changes to the code.

How Does This "Free You"? You can change the internal code without fear because you already have pop-tests that check if everything works as expected. So, you can keep improving the system without worrying about accidentally breaking things.

This also allows for faster development, since you don’t have to check every single little part of the code manually.

As your system grows, you don’t need to rewrite tests every time. You just keep running your pop-tests, and you know that new features won’t break old ones.

In short, pop-tests make engineers happier, business people more certain of what they sell and overall, it makes refactoring and maintaining things easier.

@node The Scenario DSL (Domain Specific Language)
@chapter The Scenario DSL (Domain Specific Language)

In order to provide an expressive and powerful way to describe test scenarios, a lot of care has gone into creating a good domain-specific language, which remains flexible but concise.

Here follows an explanation of every individual field and parameter possible, including default or optional ones, as well as some recommendations. Unless otherwise stated, the YAML representation of the Scenario DSL will be used.

@code{pop_test} represents the top node under which all other nodes live.

@node pop services
@chapter pop services

@code{pop_services} is a list of services to be started for the scenario.

A service is defined by:  

@code{id} (required, string) – A unique identifier for the service within the scenario. A common practice is to use a kebab-case version of the service name.  

@code{dir} (required, string) – The working directory (pwd) from which the service process should be started. This can be an absolute path or a path starting with @code{$HOME}, which will be expanded to an absolute path.  

@code{base_url} (required, string) – The URL where the service’s HTTP endpoint is reachable.  

@code{start_cmd_args} (required, list of strings) – The command(s) needed to start the service. These will be executed via a shell interpreter (default: @code{bash -c}). For example, if @code{make run} is specified, the system will actually execute @code{bash -c make run}.

@code{shell_path} (optional, string) - the path to the shell interpreter to use to run the service. This defaults to @code{bash}.  

@code{env_vars} (optional, map of string to string) – A set of environment variables to be defined for the service.  

@code{health_check} (required, object) – Defines how the system determines whether the service is healthy.  

Health checks can take different forms:

Dummy/Placebo health check:

@code{always_healthy} (boolean) – If set, forces the service to always be considered healthy (or never healthy).

HTTP health check:

@code{path} (required, string) – The HTTP route used to check the service’s health (e.g., @code{/health}).  

@code{patience_seconds} (required, integer) – The maximum time allowed for the service to become healthy. If the service is healthy before this deadline, the scenario setup continues without waiting.

@node pop checks
@chapter pop checks

@code{pop_checks} is a list of checks that validate expected responses and behaviours from the services in the scenario. These checks help verify that the services return valid and expected data before proceeding with the scenario execution.

A check is defined by:

@code{check_url} (required, string) – The endpoint path to be tested within the service. This should be a relative path, such as @code{/api/v1/forecast}, which will be appended to the base@math{_url} of the service.

@code{service_id} (required, string) – A reference to the id of the service being checked. This links the check to a specific service defined in @code{pop_services}.

@code{json_body_patterns} (optional, list of objects) – A list of conditions that must be met within the JSON response body. Each pattern defines a JSON field to inspect and a condition that must be satisfied.

Each JSON body pattern entry includes:

@code{pattern} (required, string) – A JSON path-like expression that identifies the field in the response body. This uses the Rust @code{jql} library.

@code{condition} (required, string) – A logical condition to be checked.

