@node Condition DSL
@section Condition DSL

Supported conditions include:

@code{!= ""} (ensures the field is not empty)

@code{== ""} (ensures the field is empty)

@code{> 0} (ensures the field is greater than zero)

@code{>= 0} (ensures the field is non-negative)

@code{< 0} (ensures the field is smaller than zero)

@code{<= 0} (ensures the field is non-positive)

@node pop postgres
@chapter pop postgres

You can use PostgreSQL in pop-tests easily.

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to enable PostgreSQL in a container or not

@code{image_name} (optional, string) - Container image name for PostgreSQL, by default @code{postgres}

@code{image_tag} (optional, string) - Container image tag for PostgreSQL, by default @code{17.2} or newer

@code{host_var} (optional, string) - Container hostname will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{POSTGRESQL_HOST}

@code{port_var} (optional, string) - Container DB port will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default ~POSTGRESQL@math{_PORT}

@code{db_var} (optional, string) - Container DB schema name will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{POSTGRESQL_DB}

@code{user_var} (optional, string) - Container DB username will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{POSTGRESQL_USER}

@code{pass_var} (optional, string) - Container DB password will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{POSTGRESQL_PASSWORD}

@node pop kafka
@chapter pop kafka

You can use Kafka in pop-tests easily.

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to enable Kafka in a container or not

@code{image_name} (optional, string) - Container image for Kafka, by default @code{confluentinc/cp-kafka}

@code{image_tag} (optional, string) - Container image tag for Kafka, by default @code{6.1.1}

@code{bootstrap_server_var} (optional, string) - Container HTTP connection string for Kafka will be injected on services, via environment variable, by default @code{KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVER}

@node Example tests using the DSL
@chapter Example tests, using the DSL

For example tests and learning demos, refer to the scenarios directory: @uref{../../resources/scenarios}

Here follows also a trivial example of pop-tests.

In this example we spin up a REST API written in Scala, with SBT, and poll its @code{/health} endpoint for 90 seconds or until healthy. Then we perform some checks on the response of certain HTTP endpoints. These checks are performed using @code{jql} and this allows us to check deeply nested attributes of the JSON response.

@example
pop_test:
  pop_services:
    - id: "green-energy-mix"
      dir: "$HOME/Ontwikkeling/Werk"
      base_url: "http://localhost:50000"
      start_cmd_args:
        - 'sbt "greenEnergyMix/run"'
      env_vars:
        SOME_TOKEN: "0027809ce9"
      health_check:
        path: "/health"
        patience_seconds: 90

  pop_checks:
    - check_url: "/api/v1/forecast"
      service_id: "green-energy-mix"
      json_body_patterns:
        - pattern: '"meta""forecastDate"'
          condition: '!= ""'
        - pattern: '"data"[0]"load"'
          condition: "> 0"
        - pattern: '"data"[0]"solar"'
          condition: ">= 0"

@end example

